--TEST--
A `#[Query]` on a method that declares no route is refused rather than ignored — `rule:routing/a-query-parameter-is-declared-like-a-capture`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3 gives the marker its whole meaning on a `#[Route]` method's parameter:
// the key it binds by is the parameter's own name, so away from a route there
// is nothing for it to arrive from. It is refused rather than passed over for
// the reason the compiler's roster of recognized attributes is closed at all —
// a name the compiler knows, written where the compiler never looks, reads to
// its author as a declaration that binds something.
class Users {
    public function index(#[Core\Query] string $sort): string { return $sort; }
}

// The same parameter under the route it serves is § 3's ordinary case, which
// is what makes the refusal above a fact about the missing sibling and not
// about the marker. `rule:attributes/access-is-a-required-sibling` supplies the other required sibling.
class Search {
    #[Core\Route(path: "/search", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function index(#[Core\Query] string $sort): string { return $sort; }
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0765]: `#[Query] $sort` is on a method that declares no route
  --> case.nvs:9:29
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
